# Evidence artifacts are content-addressed. Their bytes are the product's input, so git must
# not transform them.
#
# With core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default), a fresh clone converted these files from LF to
# CRLF. That changed every byte after the first line ending, which silently invalidated every
# citation in audit/claims/ and made `biasledger root` report drift for anyone who cloned on
# Windows. Verified with a clone-and-hash test.
#
# -text disables EOL conversion and text normalisation for these paths in both directions: the
# blob in the repository is byte-identical to the working tree on every platform.
#
# Note: git does not support inline `#` comments on an attribute line. Everything after the
# attribute list is parsed as another attribute, so comments live on their own lines above.
audit/evidence/** -text -diff

# The committed attestation. Its bytes are referenced by the root, and a reviewer recomputes
# that root from it offline, so it must round-trip exactly.
audit/index.json -text

# The rest of the repository normalises to LF in the repository, and CRLF in the working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF: a CRLF shebang does not execute on Unix.
*.sh text eol=lf
services/engine/src/** text eol=lf

# Windows executables keep CRLF, which is what the platform expects.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
